  "worked_solution": "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{5}^{n+1}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot {5}^{n}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{{5}^{n}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =5{(\\frac{5}{x})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{5}{x}.$ Since $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{5}{x}\\le 1\\,\u27f9\\,x\\ge 5.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
